1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,470
In less time than it takes you

2
00:00:02,470 --> 00:00:04,282
to read this sentence,

3
00:00:04,282 --> 00:00:06,671
a Formula One team can lift a

4
00:00:06,671 --> 00:00:09,554
car, change all four tyres and send

5
00:00:09,554 --> 00:00:11,366
it back into the race.

6
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:14,437
The record is one point eight seconds.

7
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:15,696
That is not luck.

8
00:00:15,876 --> 00:00:17,942
Every tool in the pit box was

9
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:19,723
designed for that number,

10
00:00:19,723 --> 00:00:21,647
from a gun that spins a nut

11
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:23,713
in a blink to the lights that

12
00:00:23,713 --> 00:00:25,280
tell the driver to go.

13
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:28,671
Today, ten parts of the F1 pit

14
00:00:28,671 --> 00:00:31,881
stop, and why each one exists.

15
00:00:32,231 --> 00:00:34,498
A pit stop is the only moment

16
00:00:34,498 --> 00:00:36,765
in a race when the car stands

17
00:00:36,765 --> 00:00:39,423
still, and every tenth of a second

18
00:00:39,423 --> 00:00:41,925
lost there is lost on the track.

19
00:00:42,105 --> 00:00:44,093
Gain one second in the pits,

20
00:00:44,093 --> 00:00:46,508
and you might jump a rival without

21
00:00:46,508 --> 00:00:47,573
overtaking him.

22
00:00:47,753 --> 00:00:50,288
So teams treat the stop like a

23
00:00:50,288 --> 00:00:52,232
sport inside the sport,

24
00:00:52,232 --> 00:00:56,372
with athletes, training and equipment that exists

25
00:00:56,372 --> 00:00:57,471
nowhere else.

26
00:00:57,821 --> 00:01:00,950
As always, every fact here comes from

27
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:02,218
a named source,

28
00:01:02,218 --> 00:01:03,995
the FIA, Formula One,

29
00:01:03,995 --> 00:01:06,870
Pirelli and motorsport references,

30
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:09,407
all listed in the description.

31
00:01:09,587 --> 00:01:11,916
Let's start with the loudest tool in

32
00:01:11,916 --> 00:01:12,757
the pit lane.

33
00:01:13,107 --> 00:01:15,714
Why can an F1 pit stop take

34
00:01:15,714 --> 00:01:17,356
only two seconds?

35
00:01:17,536 --> 00:01:19,324
Start with the wheel gun.

36
00:01:19,504 --> 00:01:21,792
It is a powerful air tool driven

37
00:01:21,792 --> 00:01:23,293
by high pressure gas,

38
00:01:23,293 --> 00:01:26,725
spinning at thousands of revolutions per minute.

39
00:01:26,905 --> 00:01:29,173
It removes the wheel nut in a

40
00:01:29,173 --> 00:01:30,815
fraction of a second,

41
00:01:30,815 --> 00:01:33,318
and tightens the new one just as

42
00:01:33,318 --> 00:01:33,709
fast.

43
00:01:33,889 --> 00:01:36,102
The gunner presses the trigger,

44
00:01:36,102 --> 00:01:38,173
and a switch on the gun tells

45
00:01:38,173 --> 00:01:40,529
the system when the nut is tight.

46
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:43,026
Each gun is a precision tool,

47
00:01:43,026 --> 00:01:45,247
built specially for the teams,

48
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:47,542
and every corner of the car has

49
00:01:47,542 --> 00:01:48,356
its gunner.

50
00:01:48,536 --> 00:01:50,803
They hold the gun against the nut

51
00:01:50,803 --> 00:01:53,413
before the car has even fully stopped.

52
00:01:53,593 --> 00:01:57,507
The gun switches direction automatically between loosening

53
00:01:57,507 --> 00:02:00,140
and tightening, so the gunner never has

54
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:01,557
to look for a button.

55
00:02:01,737 --> 00:02:03,773
The whole job of the gunner is

56
00:02:03,773 --> 00:02:05,742
about two tenths of a second,

57
00:02:05,742 --> 00:02:07,031
repeated perfectly.

58
00:02:07,382 --> 00:02:09,126
When something goes wrong,

59
00:02:09,126 --> 00:02:10,400
it is usually here.

60
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:13,042
A nut that does not engage,

61
00:02:13,042 --> 00:02:15,230
a cross threaded thread,

62
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:16,780
a gun that fails.

63
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,653
Then the stop goes from two seconds

64
00:02:19,653 --> 00:02:21,730
to ten, and the race can be

65
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:22,115
lost.

66
00:02:22,295 --> 00:02:24,146
That is why every crew has a

67
00:02:24,146 --> 00:02:26,196
spare gun ready at each corner,

68
00:02:26,196 --> 00:02:28,840
and why the gunners practise hundreds of

69
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:30,097
stops every season.

70
00:02:30,447 --> 00:02:33,369
Why does an F1 wheel have only

71
00:02:33,369 --> 00:02:36,389
one nut, when your car has four

72
00:02:36,389 --> 00:02:37,169
or five?

73
00:02:37,349 --> 00:02:40,487
Because removing five nuts would take five

74
00:02:40,487 --> 00:02:41,459
times longer.

75
00:02:41,639 --> 00:02:44,331
Racing cars use a centre lock wheel,

76
00:02:44,331 --> 00:02:46,949
one large nut in the middle holding

77
00:02:46,949 --> 00:02:49,267
the whole wheel onto a hub with

78
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:51,287
pins that carry the torque.

79
00:02:51,467 --> 00:02:54,172
One nut means one movement of the

80
00:02:54,172 --> 00:02:55,484
gun, on and off.

81
00:02:55,834 --> 00:02:58,097
The nut stays inside the wheel.

82
00:02:58,277 --> 00:02:59,736
It is a captive nut,

83
00:02:59,736 --> 00:03:01,779
so it does not fall onto the

84
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:04,040
floor when the wheel comes off,

85
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,520
and the new wheel arrives with its

86
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,343
own nut already in place.

87
00:03:08,523 --> 00:03:10,714
There is also a safety latch that

88
00:03:10,714 --> 00:03:12,440
holds the nut if it is not

89
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:13,237
fully tight.

90
00:03:13,417 --> 00:03:16,327
A lost wheel at three hundred kilometres

91
00:03:16,327 --> 00:03:18,291
per hour is one of the most

92
00:03:18,291 --> 00:03:20,545
dangerous things in motorsport.

93
00:03:20,896 --> 00:03:23,580
That danger is also why the wheels

94
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,027
are tied to the car with strong

95
00:03:26,027 --> 00:03:28,632
tethers, made of the same type of

96
00:03:28,632 --> 00:03:31,159
fibre used in bulletproof vests.

97
00:03:31,339 --> 00:03:33,908
If a crash breaks the suspension,

98
00:03:33,908 --> 00:03:36,867
the tethers keep the wheel from flying

99
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:39,826
into the crowd or into another driver.

100
00:03:40,006 --> 00:03:42,014
The one nut is the speed.

101
00:03:42,194 --> 00:03:44,718
The tethers are the safety net behind

102
00:03:44,718 --> 00:03:44,922
it.

103
00:03:45,273 --> 00:03:47,541
Why does the front jack of an

104
00:03:47,541 --> 00:03:49,731
F1 pit crew look so strange?

105
00:03:49,911 --> 00:03:53,233
The car arrives at around eighty kilometres

106
00:03:53,233 --> 00:03:55,396
per hour in the pit lane and

107
00:03:55,396 --> 00:03:57,868
stops right in front of the jack

108
00:03:57,868 --> 00:03:58,177
man.

109
00:03:58,357 --> 00:04:00,805
He slides the jack under the nose,

110
00:04:00,805 --> 00:04:03,181
and pulls the long handle down to

111
00:04:03,181 --> 00:04:05,197
lift the front of the car in

112
00:04:05,197 --> 00:04:05,845
one move.

113
00:04:06,025 --> 00:04:08,479
Then the front of the jack swivels,

114
00:04:08,479 --> 00:04:10,301
so he can step to the side

115
00:04:10,301 --> 00:04:11,703
when the car leaves.

116
00:04:12,053 --> 00:04:15,045
The jack man is the bravest person

117
00:04:15,045 --> 00:04:16,453
in the pit crew.

118
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:18,969
He stands directly in front of a

119
00:04:18,969 --> 00:04:21,305
car that is braking towards him,

120
00:04:21,305 --> 00:04:23,788
trusting the driver to stop on his

121
00:04:23,788 --> 00:04:24,226
marks.

122
00:04:24,406 --> 00:04:26,374
The jack is shaped to fit a

123
00:04:26,374 --> 00:04:28,341
specific point on the nose,

124
00:04:28,341 --> 00:04:29,653
so it cannot slip.

125
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:31,615
At the back of the car,

126
00:04:31,615 --> 00:04:34,327
another jack man does the same with

127
00:04:34,327 --> 00:04:35,257
a rear jack,

128
00:04:35,257 --> 00:04:37,426
lifting the car from behind.

129
00:04:37,777 --> 00:04:41,012
Teams have also built quick release systems

130
00:04:41,012 --> 00:04:42,140
into the jacks,

131
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:44,247
so that the car drops to the

132
00:04:44,247 --> 00:04:46,955
ground the instant the last wheel is

133
00:04:46,955 --> 00:04:47,181
on.

134
00:04:47,361 --> 00:04:50,303
Some even have a spare jack waiting

135
00:04:50,303 --> 00:04:51,311
at the side,

136
00:04:51,311 --> 00:04:53,665
in case the first one fails.

137
00:04:53,845 --> 00:04:56,274
Two seconds leaves no time to fix

138
00:04:56,274 --> 00:04:57,305
a broken tool.

139
00:04:57,485 --> 00:04:59,563
You simply grab the next one.

140
00:04:59,913 --> 00:05:02,697
Why are F1 tyres wrapped in blankets

141
00:05:02,697 --> 00:05:04,707
before they go on the car?

142
00:05:04,887 --> 00:05:08,169
Because cold racing tyres have almost no

143
00:05:08,169 --> 00:05:08,579
grip.

144
00:05:08,759 --> 00:05:11,358
These are electric heating blankets,

145
00:05:11,358 --> 00:05:13,740
and they warm the tyres for hours

146
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:15,400
before they are fitted.

147
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:18,470
A Formula One tyre only works in

148
00:05:18,470 --> 00:05:21,269
a narrow window of temperature.

149
00:05:21,449 --> 00:05:23,934
Cold, it slides like plastic.

150
00:05:24,114 --> 00:05:27,474
Warm, the rubber becomes sticky and grips

151
00:05:27,474 --> 00:05:28,293
the track.

152
00:05:28,644 --> 00:05:31,790
For years, teams heated tyres to around

153
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:33,404
one hundred degrees.

154
00:05:33,584 --> 00:05:36,731
Pirelli and the FIA have been reducing

155
00:05:36,731 --> 00:05:39,879
that limit, to around seventy degrees,

156
00:05:39,879 --> 00:05:42,778
to save energy and push tyre design

157
00:05:42,778 --> 00:05:43,440
forward.

158
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:46,523
The blankets come off seconds before the

159
00:05:46,523 --> 00:05:48,918
car arrives, so the heat stays in

160
00:05:48,918 --> 00:05:49,716
the rubber.

161
00:05:49,896 --> 00:05:52,305
The driver leaves the pits with tyres

162
00:05:52,305 --> 00:05:54,193
that are ready to grip in the

163
00:05:54,193 --> 00:05:55,039
first corner.

164
00:05:55,389 --> 00:05:58,689
Without blankets, the first lap after a

165
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:01,481
stop would be slow and dangerous,

166
00:06:01,481 --> 00:06:03,597
especially on a cold day.

167
00:06:03,777 --> 00:06:06,578
In other series that ban blankets,

168
00:06:06,578 --> 00:06:09,543
drivers often slide off the track on

169
00:06:09,543 --> 00:06:10,696
their out lap.

170
00:06:10,876 --> 00:06:13,213
That is why you see drivers weaving

171
00:06:13,213 --> 00:06:15,683
left and right behind the safety car.

172
00:06:15,863 --> 00:06:18,077
They are trying to put heat into

173
00:06:18,077 --> 00:06:20,430
the tyres when there is no blanket

174
00:06:20,430 --> 00:06:21,053
to do it.

175
00:06:21,403 --> 00:06:23,818
Why do F1 tyres have red,

176
00:06:23,818 --> 00:06:27,393
yellow or white markings on the side?

177
00:06:27,573 --> 00:06:30,139
They tell everyone which rubber the car

178
00:06:30,139 --> 00:06:30,731
is using.

179
00:06:30,911 --> 00:06:34,551
Pirelli brings three dry compounds to every

180
00:06:34,551 --> 00:06:34,975
race.

181
00:06:35,155 --> 00:06:36,339
Red is the soft,

182
00:06:36,339 --> 00:06:39,003
the fastest but the quickest to wear

183
00:06:39,003 --> 00:06:39,299
out.

184
00:06:39,479 --> 00:06:41,233
Yellow is the medium.

185
00:06:41,413 --> 00:06:42,973
White is the hard,

186
00:06:42,973 --> 00:06:45,314
slower but longest lasting.

187
00:06:45,494 --> 00:06:47,457
For rain there are two more,

188
00:06:47,457 --> 00:06:50,333
green for intermediates and blue for full

189
00:06:50,333 --> 00:06:50,683
wets.

190
00:06:51,034 --> 00:06:53,223
The colours are there for the fans

191
00:06:53,223 --> 00:06:54,447
and for the rivals.

192
00:06:54,627 --> 00:06:55,868
Under the rules,

193
00:06:55,868 --> 00:06:58,194
each car must use at least two

194
00:06:58,194 --> 00:07:01,140
different dry compounds in a dry race.

195
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:04,277
So every team watches the colours on

196
00:07:04,277 --> 00:07:07,891
its rivals' cars and guesses their strategy.

197
00:07:08,071 --> 00:07:10,835
A car on red tyres will stop

198
00:07:10,835 --> 00:07:11,624
earlier.

199
00:07:11,804 --> 00:07:14,022
A car on white will go longer

200
00:07:14,022 --> 00:07:15,705
and attack at the end.

201
00:07:16,055 --> 00:07:18,968
That rule is the hidden reason pit

202
00:07:18,968 --> 00:07:22,053
stops exist at all in modern Formula

203
00:07:22,053 --> 00:07:25,052
One, now that refuelling is banned.

204
00:07:25,232 --> 00:07:27,849
The car could perhaps finish on one

205
00:07:27,849 --> 00:07:29,195
set of hard tyres,

206
00:07:29,195 --> 00:07:31,363
but the rules make it stop at

207
00:07:31,363 --> 00:07:33,232
least once in a dry race.

208
00:07:33,412 --> 00:07:36,230
So the stop is not only about

209
00:07:36,230 --> 00:07:36,813
tyres.

210
00:07:36,993 --> 00:07:38,249
It is a chess move,

211
00:07:38,249 --> 00:07:40,431
and the colours are the pieces on

212
00:07:40,431 --> 00:07:41,092
the board.

213
00:07:41,442 --> 00:07:44,236
Why do F1 cars drive so slowly

214
00:07:44,236 --> 00:07:45,726
in the pit lane?

215
00:07:45,906 --> 00:07:47,700
There is a speed limit,

216
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:50,430
usually eighty kilometres per hour,

217
00:07:50,430 --> 00:07:52,848
and lower on some tight tracks.

218
00:07:53,028 --> 00:07:55,605
Mechanics are standing in the lane,

219
00:07:55,605 --> 00:07:58,256
with no barrier between them and the

220
00:07:58,256 --> 00:07:58,624
cars.

221
00:07:58,804 --> 00:08:01,453
At the white line at the entrance,

222
00:08:01,453 --> 00:08:04,102
the driver presses a button on the

223
00:08:04,102 --> 00:08:07,140
steering wheel, and a limiter holds the

224
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,555
car exactly at the speed limit.

225
00:08:09,906 --> 00:08:12,725
The limit is checked electronically,

226
00:08:12,725 --> 00:08:15,622
and going even one kilometre per hour

227
00:08:15,622 --> 00:08:17,580
too fast earns a penalty.

228
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:20,198
That is why drivers brake very late

229
00:08:20,198 --> 00:08:23,124
before the line and accelerate the instant

230
00:08:23,124 --> 00:08:24,865
they cross the exit line.

231
00:08:25,045 --> 00:08:27,261
Every metre of the pit lane is

232
00:08:27,261 --> 00:08:27,704
timed.

233
00:08:27,884 --> 00:08:30,542
Some tracks have long pit lanes,

234
00:08:30,542 --> 00:08:33,366
and a stop there costs twenty five

235
00:08:33,366 --> 00:08:35,442
seconds or more in total,

236
00:08:35,442 --> 00:08:38,100
even if the tyres change in two.

237
00:08:38,451 --> 00:08:41,543
Speed limits in the pit lane exist

238
00:08:41,543 --> 00:08:43,907
because of real accidents.

239
00:08:44,087 --> 00:08:45,017
In the past,

240
00:08:45,017 --> 00:08:47,731
cars entered at full speed and crew

241
00:08:47,731 --> 00:08:49,126
members were hurt.

242
00:08:49,306 --> 00:08:51,356
Today the pit lane is one of

243
00:08:51,356 --> 00:08:53,773
the safest places on the circuit,

244
00:08:53,773 --> 00:08:55,970
and the limiter is the reason.

245
00:08:56,150 --> 00:08:59,695
A machine built for three hundred kilometres

246
00:08:59,695 --> 00:09:02,515
per hour, crawling past people at a

247
00:09:02,515 --> 00:09:04,207
fixed speed, by rule.

248
00:09:04,558 --> 00:09:06,900
Why is there a traffic light hanging

249
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:08,006
over the pit box?

250
00:09:08,186 --> 00:09:10,194
It tells the driver when to go.

251
00:09:10,374 --> 00:09:13,264
Each wheel crew presses a button when

252
00:09:13,264 --> 00:09:14,670
its wheel is done.

253
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:17,430
When all four signals are in and

254
00:09:17,430 --> 00:09:18,881
the lane is clear,

255
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:20,655
the light turns green.

256
00:09:20,835 --> 00:09:22,425
Before these systems,

257
00:09:22,425 --> 00:09:24,091
a man held a sign on a

258
00:09:24,091 --> 00:09:26,287
stick in front of the driver,

259
00:09:26,287 --> 00:09:27,802
called the lollipop,

260
00:09:27,802 --> 00:09:30,150
and lifted it when the stop was

261
00:09:30,150 --> 00:09:30,831
finished.

262
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:34,564
Lights are faster than any human reaction,

263
00:09:34,564 --> 00:09:37,705
and every hundredth of a second counts.

264
00:09:37,885 --> 00:09:39,720
But they made a famous mistake.

265
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:42,503
At the Singapore Grand Prix of two

266
00:09:42,503 --> 00:09:46,025
thousand eight, Felipe Massa's Ferrari got the

267
00:09:46,025 --> 00:09:48,476
signal to go while the fuel hose

268
00:09:48,476 --> 00:09:49,931
was still attached.

269
00:09:50,111 --> 00:09:52,746
He drove off dragging the hose down

270
00:09:52,746 --> 00:09:53,725
the pit lane,

271
00:09:53,725 --> 00:09:55,533
and his race was ruined.

272
00:09:55,882 --> 00:09:58,518
Releasing a car into the path of

273
00:09:58,518 --> 00:10:01,812
another one is called an unsafe release,

274
00:10:01,812 --> 00:10:03,789
and it brings a penalty.

275
00:10:03,969 --> 00:10:06,596
Some teams have a person who watches

276
00:10:06,596 --> 00:10:09,077
the lane and can stop the release.

277
00:10:09,257 --> 00:10:11,114
The traffic light is fast,

278
00:10:11,114 --> 00:10:14,113
but the final decision often still depends

279
00:10:14,113 --> 00:10:16,399
on one person looking at the pit

280
00:10:16,399 --> 00:10:19,184
lane, ready to keep the driver waiting.

281
00:10:19,534 --> 00:10:22,124
Why don't F1 cars refuel during the

282
00:10:22,124 --> 00:10:23,086
race anymore?

283
00:10:23,266 --> 00:10:25,902
Refuelling was banned from twenty ten.

284
00:10:26,082 --> 00:10:29,805
Before that, from nineteen ninety-four to two

285
00:10:29,805 --> 00:10:33,198
thousand nine, pit stops included a giant

286
00:10:33,198 --> 00:10:36,756
fuel hose, pushing around twelve litres per

287
00:10:36,756 --> 00:10:38,411
second into the car.

288
00:10:38,591 --> 00:10:41,424
A stop took seven or eight seconds,

289
00:10:41,424 --> 00:10:44,176
and the fuel decided the strategy.

290
00:10:44,526 --> 00:10:45,814
It was dangerous.

291
00:10:45,994 --> 00:10:47,892
In nineteen ninety-four,

292
00:10:47,892 --> 00:10:51,768
at Hockenheim, fuel sprayed over Jos Verstappen's

293
00:10:51,768 --> 00:10:54,853
Benetton during a stop and caught fire.

294
00:10:55,033 --> 00:10:57,491
The car and crew were engulfed in

295
00:10:57,491 --> 00:10:59,502
flames for several seconds.

296
00:10:59,682 --> 00:11:01,145
The crew survived,

297
00:11:01,145 --> 00:11:02,527
with minor burns.

298
00:11:02,707 --> 00:11:05,680
There were other fires over the years,

299
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:08,184
and hoses that stayed connected,

300
00:11:08,184 --> 00:11:10,219
like Massa's in Singapore.

301
00:11:10,569 --> 00:11:13,543
The ban was also about cost and

302
00:11:13,543 --> 00:11:14,214
racing.

303
00:11:14,394 --> 00:11:18,369
Carrying heavy equipment around the world was

304
00:11:18,369 --> 00:11:22,344
expensive, and many overtakes happened in the

305
00:11:22,344 --> 00:11:24,553
pits instead of on track.

306
00:11:24,733 --> 00:11:27,461
Today, F1 cars start the race with

307
00:11:27,461 --> 00:11:29,868
all the fuel they need for the

308
00:11:29,868 --> 00:11:31,072
whole distance.

309
00:11:31,252 --> 00:11:34,521
That is why tyre changes became so

310
00:11:34,521 --> 00:11:35,002
fast.

311
00:11:35,182 --> 00:11:37,540
There is nothing else left to wait

312
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:37,817
for.

313
00:11:38,168 --> 00:11:40,346
Why does one F1 pit stop need

314
00:11:40,346 --> 00:11:41,848
about twenty people?

315
00:11:42,028 --> 00:11:45,316
Because each person does only one tiny

316
00:11:45,316 --> 00:11:45,662
job.

317
00:11:45,842 --> 00:11:48,182
At each wheel there are three,

318
00:11:48,182 --> 00:11:50,445
a gunner, one to pull the old

319
00:11:50,445 --> 00:11:52,629
wheel off and one to put the

320
00:11:52,629 --> 00:11:53,644
new wheel on.

321
00:11:53,824 --> 00:11:55,256
Then two jack men,

322
00:11:55,256 --> 00:11:57,644
people holding the car steady,

323
00:11:57,644 --> 00:11:59,793
someone for the front wing,

324
00:11:59,793 --> 00:12:02,659
and the person watching the release.

325
00:12:02,839 --> 00:12:04,801
Nobody does two things.

326
00:12:05,151 --> 00:12:07,231
Most of them are not full time

327
00:12:07,231 --> 00:12:07,856
pit crew.

328
00:12:08,036 --> 00:12:10,737
They are mechanics who build and fix

329
00:12:10,737 --> 00:12:12,237
the car all weekend,

330
00:12:12,237 --> 00:12:14,788
and switch to their pit stop roles

331
00:12:14,788 --> 00:12:15,988
during the race.

332
00:12:16,168 --> 00:12:18,172
They train like athletes,

333
00:12:18,172 --> 00:12:21,859
with fitness programs and hundreds of practice

334
00:12:21,859 --> 00:12:23,542
stops at the factory,

335
00:12:23,542 --> 00:12:26,107
measured with sensors and video.

336
00:12:26,287 --> 00:12:28,568
A tenth of a second is found

337
00:12:28,568 --> 00:12:30,687
in the position of a foot.

338
00:12:31,037 --> 00:12:34,197
The fastest stop ever recorded in Formula

339
00:12:34,197 --> 00:12:36,664
One was one point eight seconds,

340
00:12:36,664 --> 00:12:39,284
by McLaren for Lando Norris at the

341
00:12:39,284 --> 00:12:42,368
Qatar Grand Prix of twenty twenty-three.

342
00:12:42,548 --> 00:12:45,328
Before that, Red Bull held the record

343
00:12:45,328 --> 00:12:47,807
with one point eight two seconds.

344
00:12:47,987 --> 00:12:49,798
These are not lucky stops.

345
00:12:49,978 --> 00:12:52,457
They are the result of twenty people

346
00:12:52,457 --> 00:12:53,972
moving as one machine.

347
00:12:54,322 --> 00:12:56,888
Why do F1 teams still hold out

348
00:12:56,888 --> 00:12:59,367
a sign board on the pit wall,

349
00:12:59,367 --> 00:13:01,590
when drivers have a radio?

350
00:13:01,770 --> 00:13:03,698
Because radios fail.

351
00:13:03,878 --> 00:13:06,372
The pit board shows the driver his

352
00:13:06,372 --> 00:13:09,014
position, the gap to the cars around

353
00:13:09,014 --> 00:13:10,995
him and the laps remaining.

354
00:13:11,175 --> 00:13:13,977
It is the oldest communication tool in

355
00:13:13,977 --> 00:13:17,001
racing, and it works when everything else

356
00:13:17,001 --> 00:13:17,664
does not.

357
00:13:18,015 --> 00:13:21,672
Radio problems are common in Formula One.

358
00:13:21,852 --> 00:13:24,307
Interference, broken connectors,

359
00:13:24,307 --> 00:13:26,916
or simply a driver who cannot hear

360
00:13:26,916 --> 00:13:28,144
over the engine.

361
00:13:28,324 --> 00:13:29,594
When that happens,

362
00:13:29,594 --> 00:13:31,640
the pit board is the only way

363
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:33,474
to tell him to come in for

364
00:13:33,474 --> 00:13:33,897
tyres.

365
00:13:34,247 --> 00:13:36,294
Think about it the next time you

366
00:13:36,294 --> 00:13:36,998
see a stop.

367
00:13:37,178 --> 00:13:39,883
A board from the nineteen fifties,

368
00:13:39,883 --> 00:13:43,304
a gun spinning at thousands of revolutions,

369
00:13:43,304 --> 00:13:47,361
electric blankets, traffic lights and twenty people

370
00:13:47,361 --> 00:13:48,475
moving as one.

371
00:13:48,655 --> 00:13:51,093
A two second pit stop is the

372
00:13:51,093 --> 00:13:54,227
most practised two seconds in all of

373
00:13:54,227 --> 00:13:54,750
sport.

374
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:56,411
So here are the ten.

375
00:13:56,591 --> 00:13:58,871
A wheel gun that works in a

376
00:13:58,871 --> 00:13:59,378
blink.

377
00:13:59,558 --> 00:14:01,485
One nut instead of five.

378
00:14:01,665 --> 00:14:03,813
A jack that swivels away.

379
00:14:03,993 --> 00:14:06,319
Blankets that heat the tyres,

380
00:14:06,319 --> 00:14:08,806
colours that show the strategy,

381
00:14:08,806 --> 00:14:11,935
a speed limiter that protects the crew,

382
00:14:11,935 --> 00:14:14,181
lights that release the car,

383
00:14:14,181 --> 00:14:16,428
a fuel hose that was banned,

384
00:14:16,428 --> 00:14:18,995
twenty people with one job each,

385
00:14:18,995 --> 00:14:21,482
and a board that works when the

386
00:14:21,482 --> 00:14:22,685
radio does not.

387
00:14:23,035 --> 00:14:25,191
A pit stop looks like chaos,

388
00:14:25,191 --> 00:14:27,116
but it is one of the most

389
00:14:27,116 --> 00:14:29,118
designed moments in sport.

390
00:14:29,298 --> 00:14:32,329
If you want more machines explained part

391
00:14:32,329 --> 00:14:35,208
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

392
00:14:35,388 --> 00:14:37,287
And tell us in the comments which

393
00:14:37,287 --> 00:14:39,187
machine you want us to take apart

394
00:14:39,187 --> 00:14:39,474
next.
